p.src:
Because the bad equilibrium relies on the incentive to rush for the exit before others do. If depositors believe that even a failing bank will repay them, the incentive to run first is weakened. So deposit insurance is not merely ex post compensation. It is an ex ante device for altering expectations: $\text{insured deposits}
\Rightarrow
\text{less incentive to withdraw early}
\Rightarrow
\text{run prevention}.$ That is why the lecture presents Diamond–Dybvig not only as a theory of bank runs, but also as an economic rationale for deposit insurance.

